  === reason_with_thessalon

  "Thessalon!" you shout over the chanting. "Stop! You don't understand what you're unleashing!"

  The Archmage's head snaps toward you, his chanting faltering. His eyes—Velara was right—are pools of absolute darkness, reflecting the void within the Eye. For a moment, recognition flickers across his face.

  "You..." he whispers. "You're from the family. They sent you." His laugh is bitter. "Do you know what they did? What they imprisoned in this 'heirloom' of theirs?"

  The Eye pulses, and Thessalon's expression twists with pain—or ecstasy, you can't tell which.

  "It was my daughter," he says. "Three hundred years ago, your ancestors imprisoned my daughter's soul to power their estate's wards. When I discovered the truth, they'd already forgotten what the Tear truly was. Forgotten that they'd stolen someone's life to fuel their comfort."

  The revelation stuns you. If he's telling the truth...

  * [Help him complete the ritual and free his daughter]
    ~ helped_thessalon = true
    ~ family_betrayed = true
    -> help_ritual

  * [Stop him—you can't trust that what's in the Eye is still his daughter]
    ~ opposed_thessalon = true
    -> oppose_ritual

  * [Ask for proof—you need to know the truth before deciding]
    ~ truth_seeking = true
    -> demand_proof

  === help_ritual

  "Tell me what to do," you say, stepping into the circle. "If what you say is true, then my family's debt is older and darker than I knew."

  Thessalon's expression shifts from suspicion to desperate hope. "Stand opposite me. When I reach the final phrase, channel your will into breaking the seal. The Eye will resist—it's been a prison for so long, it's forgotten how to be anything else."

  You take your position. The ritual resumes, and this time you add your voice to his. The Eye cracks further, light bleeding through—not the light of stars, but something warmer. More human.

  The seal shatters.

  What emerges is not a monster, but a woman—translucent, confused, her form flickering between substance and spirit. She looks at Thessalon with ancient recognition.

  "Father?" she whispers. "How long has it been?"

  "Too long, Lira," Thessalon says, tears streaming down his face. "Far too long."

  He turns to you, and in his eyes you see the darkness receding. "Thank you. You could have killed us both, but you chose mercy. What will you tell your family?"

  * [Tell them the truth—let them face their ancestors' crime]
    ~ ending = "truth_teller"
    -> ending_truth

  * [Lie and say Thessalon destroyed the Tear]
    ~ ending = "merciful_liar"
    -> ending_lie

  * [Demand payment for your help—you came here for compensation]
    ~ ending = "mercenary_helper"
    -> ending_payment

  === oppose_ritual

  "I can't let you do this!" you shout, diving forward to disrupt the ritual circle. Whether Thessalon speaks truth or lies, releasing something imprisoned for three centuries is too dangerous a gamble.

  Thessalon roars in fury, and magical force slams into you like a hammer. You crash against the observatory wall, ribs cracking. But the circle is broken, the ritual disrupted.

  The Eye shrieks—an impossible sound that shouldn't come from an object. It begins to collapse inward, imploding under its own power.

  "No!" Thessalon screams. "Lira! I'm so sorry, I'm so sorry—"

  The Eye vanishes with a sound like breaking glass. Thessalon collapses, sobbing. When he looks up at you, his eyes are normal again—human, brown, filled with absolute grief.

  "She's gone," he whispers. "Three hundred years I searched, and you destroyed her in a moment."

  * [Try to explain that you thought it was a monster]
    -> ending_regret

  * [Stand your ground—you made the right choice given what you knew]
    -> ending_pragmatic

  * [Offer him mercy or a quick death]
    -> ending_merciful_end

  === destroy_tear

  You don't waste time with words or hesitation. Drawing on every scrap of magical power you possess, you unleash a blast of pure force at the Eye.

  The Eye shatters.

  What happens next happens fast: the observatory fills with blinding light and deafening sound. Thessalon screams—in rage or anguish, you can't tell. The tower itself shudders, wards collapsing, centuries of accumulated magic bleeding out into the night.

  When the light fades, you're alone. Thessalon is gone—fled, dead, or transformed, you'll never know. The Eye's fragments litter the floor like teardrops made of night.

  As you examine them, you hear a whisper—a woman's voice, fading into nothing:

  "Thank you... for ending it..."

  The fragments dissolve into mist.

  ~ tear_destroyed = true
  ~ thessalon_fate_unknown = true

  * [Take proof of the Tear's destruction back to your family]
    -> ending_mission_complete

  * [Leave everything and disappear—you want no part of this anymore]
    -> ending_walk_away

  === ending_truth

  You return to your family with the truth. The revelation that the Tear of Luminous contained a imprisoned soul—and that your ancestors were responsible—tears the family apart. Some refuse to believe. Others demand you be punished for betraying the family contract.

  But a few—the younger generation—stand with you. The family's fortune was built on suffering. It's time to make amends.

  Years later, you stand before a memorial you helped erect: a simple stone marked with Lira's name, and an apology centuries overdue. Thessalon never returned to claim it, but sometimes you find fresh moonflowers placed at its base.

  You sleep better now than you have in years.

  ENDING: The Truth Shall Make You Free

  -> END

  === ending_lie

  You return to your family and spin a tale: Thessalon destroyed the Tear in his mad ritual before you could stop him. The family is disappointed but pays your fee. You don't tell them what the Tear truly was.

  Sometimes, late at night, you wonder if you made the right choice. The family still prospers on the foundation of old sins. But Lira is free, and Thessalon has his daughter back—ghostly and strange though she may be.

  One evening, you find a letter on your doorstep. No signature, just two words in elegant script: "Thank you."

  You burn it immediately, but you keep the memory.

  ENDING: Merciful Deceiver

  -> END

  === ending_payment

  "I came here to get paid," you say bluntly. "The truth doesn't change that. Your daughter is free, but I still did a job—just not the one I was hired for."

  Thessalon studies you for a long moment, then laughs—a sound of genuine amusement. "You're honest, at least. I can respect that." He produces a bag of gems and rare magical components. "This should more than cover your fee. And you'll keep my secret?"

  "The family never needs to know the Tear still exists," you agree. "As far as they're concerned, you destroyed it."

  "Then we have an accord."

  You leave the tower wealthier than you arrived, and with a strange new understanding: sometimes the monsters are those who hire you, not those you're sent to stop.

  ENDING: Professional Standards

  -> END

  === ending_regret

  "I thought—I believed you were unleashing something terrible," you say quietly. "If I had known..."

  "But you didn't know," Thessalon says, his voice hollow. "You acted on fear and assumption. And now she's gone forever."

  He stands slowly, looking older than his years. "I won't kill you. You were ignorant, not malicious. But leave. Leave and never return to this tower. And tell your family..." He pauses. "Tell them nothing. Let them wonder what happened to their stolen prize."

  You leave with the weight of your mistake crushing your chest. For the rest of your life, you'll wonder if you destroyed a monster or murdered an innocent. You'll never know for certain.

  ENDING: The Price of Ignorance

  -> END

  === ending_pragmatic

  "I made the only choice I could," you say firmly. "You were an Archmage performing a dark ritual with a clearly cursed object. Whatever was in that Eye—daughter, demon, or something worse—keeping it imprisoned was the safest option."

  Thessalon looks at you with empty eyes. "Safe. Yes. Always so concerned with safety, you people. Never with justice. Never with mercy." He waves a hand dismissively. "Go. Tell your family their precious Tear is destroyed. Collect your fee. Sleep soundly knowing you did the 'right thing.'"

  His voice drips with bitterness, but he doesn't attack. You leave, and though you stand by your choice, the doubt never fully fades.

  ENDING: Necessary Evil

  -> END

  === ending_merciful_end

  You draw your blade. "I can give you mercy, if you want it. A quick death, no more suffering."

  Thessalon looks up at you, and for a moment you think he'll accept. Then he laughs—a broken, terrible sound. "You've already killed her. Killing me would be redundant." He stands, power crackling around his hands. "But if you want to try, then try."

  * [Fight him to the death]
    -> ending_final_duel

  * [Lower your weapon and leave]
    -> ending_walk_away_mercy

  === ending_final_duel

  The battle is brief and brutal. Thessalon, grief-mad and channeling power with suicidal abandon, nearly obliterates you in the first exchange. But grief makes him reckless, and you're fighting for your life.

  Your blade finds his heart.

  As he dies, he whispers: "Lira... I'm coming..."

  You leave the tower as it begins to collapse, the Archmage's death unraveling three centuries of magical infrastructure. Behind you, the Tower of Sorrows falls, and with it, all the secrets it contained.

  Your family pays you. You never tell them what really happened. Some truths are too heavy to share.

  ENDING: Pyrrhic Victory

  -> END

  === ending_walk_away_mercy

  You lower your weapon. "I won't kill you. You've suffered enough."

  Thessalon says nothing, just turns away. You leave him there, a broken man in a tower of broken dreams.

  You never learn what became of him. The Tower of Sorrows stands for another year, then one day it's simply... gone. No explosion, no collapse. Just empty land where the tower once stood.

  You tell yourself he found peace somehow. You want to believe that.

  ENDING: Uncertain Mercy

  -> END

  === ending_mission_complete

  You return to your family with fragments of the Eye as proof of its destruction. They're satisfied—the curse is broken, the heirloom reclaimed (or destroyed, which amounts to the same thing). You're paid handsomely.

  But sometimes, in dreams, you hear a woman's voice thanking you. You never know if you freed her or destroyed her. Perhaps it was both.

  You use your payment to start a new life far from your family. Some legacies are too dark to inherit.

  ENDING: Mission Accomplished

  -> END

  === ending_walk_away

  You leave the fragments where they lie and walk out of the Tower of Sorrows. You keep walking—out of the city, out of the province, away from your family and their cursed heirlooms and their blood-soaked legacy.

  Somewhere in the wilderness, you stop and look back. The tower stands silent against the dawn sky. You'll never know the full truth of what happened there, and you've decided you don't want to.

  Some mysteries are better left unsolved. Some families are better left behind.

  ENDING: The Wanderer

  -> END

